WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.550
Filmmakers love to throw all kinds of extra
treats into their movies for eagle-eyed fans.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:14.950
Nicknamed "Easter eggs" because they're just
barely hidden and fun to find, these inside

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:20.250
jokes, visual gags, and pop culture references
add another level of enjoyment to the viewing

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.430
experience.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.990
Viewers and fans discover most of them right
away, but others are a little too well-hidden.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:32.189
Take these Easter eggs, for example: years
went by before anybody noticed them.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.570
The Topless Woman In The Rescuers

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:40.920
In this 1977 animated Disney movie, the titular
mice Bernard and Bianca take flight on the

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.420
back of a bird.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.670
As they zoom past buildings, a still image
of a naked woman appears in a window in two

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.489
different, non-consecutive frames.

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:54.339
Nobody noticed the fraction of a second’s
worth of nudity — placed by a troublemaker

00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:58.719
in a post-production house — during the
theatrical release, and a different print

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:02.550
was used when The Rescuers was first released
on VHS.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.689
When the movie was re-released on tape in
1999, better VHS players and their pausing

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:11.200
capabilities led to the Easter egg’s discovery.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.829
The news spread, and Disney recalled three
million videos.

00:01:15.829 --> 00:01:19.119
Frisky Wizards In Harry Potter And The Prisoner
Of Azkaban

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:23.299
The end credits of the third Harry Potter
movie involve the Marauder's Map detailing

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:27.789
the comings and goings of all Hogwart's personnel
in the form of footprints.

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:33.390
Amidst all the steps going to and fro, there's
a brief bit in the corner depicting two pairs

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.990
of footprints very close together—and facing
each other.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.259
Yep: Those were a couple of teenage wizards
makin' some real magic with each other.

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:48.430
It wasn’t noticed until 2011, seven years
after the film's theatrical release.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.990
Waldo In Apocalypto

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:55.140
Mel Gibson's directorial follow-up to The
Passion of the Christ was 2006's Apocalypto,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.220
a violent drama about the fall of the Mayan
empire.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.560
One of the most striking and haunting images
of the film is a mass grave of fallen Mayans.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.890
For some reason, Gibson inserted a single
frame of an actor dressed as Waldo.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.600
As in “Where’s Waldo.”

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.170
The Easter egg was only present in the theatrical
release of the movie and not the home video

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:22.470
version, which meant this bizarre extra wasn't
discovered until bootlegs of the original

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.750
cut surfaced.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.130
Jack Skellington In Beetlejuice

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.340
In Tim Burton's Beetlejuice, there's a scene
in which the ghost with the most rises out

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.650
of the town model, his head spinning.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:36.940
Pay attention, and you'll see a creepy little
skull on the top of his head.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:42.170
We obviously had no way of knowing at the
time, but that skull was the prototype for

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:47.480
Jack Skellington, the main character in Tim
Burton's stop-motion animated movie The Nightmare

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.540
Before Christmas, released five years after
Beetlejuice.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:52.540
Thanks for watching!

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.230
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:00.230
And leave us a comment to let us know what
Easter eggs you’ve spotted...that no one

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:00.430
else has.

